1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
In this lesson, we'll build an agent from scratch.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
You'll see that while agents can do fairly complex tasks,

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
a basic agent is actually not that hard to build.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
As we're building this agent, it will be useful to notice

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
what jobs fall to the LOM,

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
and which are managed by the code around the LOM,

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
what we call the runtime.

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Let's dive in.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
The agent we're going to build from scratch

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
is based on the React pattern.

11
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
React stands for reasoning plus acting.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
In this pattern, an LOM first thinks about what to do,

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
and then decides an action to take.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
That action is then executed in an environment

15
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
and an observation is returned.

16
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
With that observation, the LOM then repeats.

17
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Then thinks about what to do again,

18
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
decides another action to take,

19
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
and continues until it decides that it is done.

20
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The code that we will be using to start with

21
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
is based on this blog post from Simon Wilson.

22
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
It's a great introduction to implementing the React pattern in Python.

23
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
To get started, let's import everything we need.

24
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
We can then initialize the language model.

25
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
For this, we will use OpenAI.

26
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Let's try it out once just to make sure that it's working.

27
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
We can see that we pass in Hello World and get back.

28
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Hello, how can I assist you today?

29
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Great.

30
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
We're set up and running with our language model.

31
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Let's now create the agent.

32
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
We'll start by creating a class for the agent.

33
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
We'll want this agent to be parameterized by a system message,

34
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
so we'll allow the user to pass that message.

35
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
So we'll allow the user to pass that in.

36
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
We'll then save that as an attribute.

37
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
We'll also keep track of a list of messages over time.

38
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
This is where we will append everything that happens in that React loop.

39
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
And to start it off, if there is this message,

40
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,000
we're going to append a system message in this list of messages.

41
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Okay, great.

42
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Sorry, agent can now be initialized.

43
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
But what exactly is it going to do?

44
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Let's implement the call method for this agent.

45
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
What we want this method to do at a high level is to take a message that comes in,

46
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
that is a string, and then append that message to the existing array of messages.

47
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
We'll then execute a function.

48
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
We'll implement that right down below.

49
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
And we'll then take the result of that and add a new message into the array of messages.

50
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
This time coming from the assistant.

51
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
So let's implement that execute method.

52
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
In the execute method, this is where we call the opening client that we initialized above.

53
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
So we'll be using this GPT4 model.

54
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
We'll be setting temperature equals to zero.

55
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
This will make it very deterministic.

56
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
And we'll pass in this list of messages that will accumulate.

57
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:19,000
We'll then have this method return the content or the string of the message that it gets back from the model.

58
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,000
So this is our agent.

59
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Awesome.

60
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Now let's get started with creating the React agent.

61
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
The React agent is going to require a very specific system message.

62
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
So let's go ahead and write that out.

63
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,000
And we can take a look at that in detail to see exactly what we're doing.

64
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Let's take a look at this prompt in more detail.

65
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So we're asking it to run through this loop of thought, action, pause, and observation.

66
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
It can then output an answer when it's finished with that loop.

67
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
It will use thought to describe its thoughts about the question it's been asked.

68
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,000
It will then use action to run one of the actions available to it.

69
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:05,000
And it will then return pause after that observation will be used to signal the result of running those actions.

70
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
We then tell it what the available actions are.

71
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,000
So we give it access to calculate an average dog weight and we'll implement those down below.

72
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Finally, we then provide an example of this in action.

73
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
This example is really helpful for helping the language model understand

74
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
in a little bit more specific detail how exactly it should be doing things.

75
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:34,000
And we can see here an example trace incoming question and then a thought and then an action, then pause.

76
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
And then it gets back an observation and then we'll tell it to output answer.

77
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
We now need to provide the two tools that we mentioned above.

78
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
So we have a calculate function and this just takes in a string and it will evolve that string.

79
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,000
And then we have this average dog weight function.

80
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
And we're going to mock it with some returns for Scottish Terrier border call a toy poodle.

81
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
And then we'll create this little dictionary that maps the name of the function to the function.

82
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
And we'll see how we'll use this down below.

83
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Note that these functions are just a toy example.

84
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
And in your real use case, these are going to be more specific to the problem that you're trying to solve.

85
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Let's now try it out.

86
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Let's initialize the agent with the prompt that we created above.

87
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Let's then call it once with this initial question.

88
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
How much does a toy poodle weigh?

89
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
We look at the result and we can see that there's a thought action and pause.

90
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,000
So the thought is I should look up the dog's weight using average dog weight for a toy poodle.

91
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
It then outputs an action and this is all one string but the second part is outputting this action.

92
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Average dog weight toy poodle and then it says pause.

93
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,000
What this means we should do is that we should look up using average dog weight, how much a toy poodle weighs.

94
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
And so let's do that.

95
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
We get back this string and this is just what we mocked out above.

96
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
We can now format that into the next prompt to pass to the language model.

97
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
And then we can call the agent with that next prompt.

98
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Doing that we see that we get back answer a toy poodle weighs seven pounds.

99
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:33,000
If we want to see more details about what exactly has been going on, we can take a look at the message attribute on the agent.

100
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:40,000
We can see that we first have this system message and this is the long prompt that we wrote out above.

101
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
We then have our first question, how much does a toy poodle weigh?

102
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
We then have this response from the language model, which tells us to take this action.

103
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
We then have our user message representing this observation, saying how much a toy poodle weighs.

104
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:02,000
And then we have the final assistant response, which says our final answer to the toy poodle weighs seven pounds.

105
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Let's try this again with a different example.

106
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:13,000
In order to do that, we're going to need to reinitialize the agent in order to clear all the messages that have already accumulated.

107
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
We're going to ask it a more complicated question this time.

108
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:20,000
We have two dogs, a border collie and a Scottish terrier.

109
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
What is their combined weight?

110
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
We can see that's first thought is a really good plan.

111
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:32,000
It realizes that it first needs to find the average weight of each breed and then add those weights together.

112
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:40,000
And so the first action that it takes is calling average dog weight with border collie.

113
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Let's execute that action and create the next prompt.

114
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
I can now call the agent on this next prompt.

115
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
I can see that it responds that we now need to take a different action.

116
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
We need to call average dog weight with Scottish terrier.

117
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
So let's do that.

118
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:02,000
We do that and create another prompt, which we can again pass to the agent.

119
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:08,000
This time it tells us to use the calculate method with 37 plus 20.

120
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,000
Okay, great.

121
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Let's run that action.

122
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:20,000
We run that action and we get back another prompt, which we can pass yet again into the agent.

123
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Doing that, we get back a final answer.

124
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:26,000
The combined weight of a border collie and a Scottish terrier is 57 pounds.

125
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Now that is output the answer string, we know that it's finished.

126
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,000
So this is great.

127
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,000
We have a tool calling agent, but it's still a little bit manual.

128
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Let's automate it.

129
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Let's put it in a loop.

130
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:46,000
The first thing I'm going to do is create this reg X for looking for the action string.

131
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:52,000
This is going to let us parse the LLM's response and determine whether we want to be taking an action

132
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,000
or whether it's the final answer.

133
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Let's now create this query function.

134
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:04,000
This is going to take in a question and run the same process that we just ran manually.

135
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
I'll add this max turns per emitter so we can control how long this runs for.

136
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:18,000
We can then start a counter to keep track of how many iterations we've done.

137
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:26,000
We can initialize an agent with the default system prompt.

138
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:30,000
We can then define a query function, which we'll use to run this loop.

139
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,000
It'll take in a question and we'll also let it take in this max turns parameter,

140
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
which will control how many iterations the agent can run for.

141
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
We'll start a counter and we'll set this to zero.

142
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:46,000
We'll create a new agent by initializing the agent class with the default system prompt.

143
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:51,000
We'll also create this next prompt variable to keep track of what we should be passing to the agent

144
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:55,000
and set this equal to the original question in order to start.

145
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:05,000
Let's now start our loop.

146
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,000
So while our counter is less than max turns, we'll first increment the counter

147
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:15,000
and then we'll call the agent and get a result back.

148
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Let's add a print statement just to see what that result is.

149
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:27,000
We'll then use the reg X that we created above in order to parse the response from the agent

150
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:32,000
and we'll get back a list of actions.

151
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Here's where we will decide what to do based on the response.

152
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:44,000
So if there are any actions, we're going to add some logic to take those actions and get back a response.

153
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:51,000
If there aren't any actions, and this happens when the agent outputs answer, then we're going to return and end the function there.

154
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:57,000
So what exactly are we going to do if there are actions to take?

155
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:01,000
First, we're going to get the action and the action input.

156
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:06,000
The action is the function to call and the action input is the input to that function.

157
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
So we'll get those two things.

158
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:13,000
And then if we see an action that's not in our list of known actions, we're going to raise an exception.

159
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:18,000
This should never happen, but we'll do this check and raise this exception just in case.

160
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:22,000
And let's print out what exactly we're going to be running here.

161
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
We'll now get the observation.

162
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:33,000
And so we'll do this by first looking up in the action dictionary, the action that we should take.

163
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
And so this action is a string right here.

164
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
We're looking it up in this dictionary and we're going to get back a function.

165
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
We're then calling that function on the action input.

166
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:47,000
And the result of that is what we're saving as observation.

167
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Let's print out the observation just for some debugging.

168
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And then let's also create the next prompt.

169
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
What we'll send to the language model the next time.

170
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:03,000
And this is just a string with observation and formatted with the observation that we got back from the agent.

171
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:11,000
All right, let's call this on the complex question that we had above and see what happens.

172
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:21,000
We can see that at first thinks about what to do outputs an action calls it gets back an observation thinks about what to do again gets another action thinks about what to do again gets another action.

173
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
And then finally it comes up with this answer.

174
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,000
That was a lot easier than running it by hand.

175
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:35,000
That shows how we can create an agent using nothing but the raw LLM API and some Python code.

176
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:41,000
In the next lesson, we'll take the same agent and show how to implement it using length graph.

177
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:42,000
See you there.
